A range loop counts through numbers.

`to` stops before the end value:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0 to 5 |number|:
        io.line([: [number]])
    ;
]

This produces `0`, `1`, `2`, `3` and `4`.

Use `to &` when the end value should be included:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0 to & 5 |number|:
        io.line([: [number]])
    ;
]

This also produces `5`.

### Start from zero

You can leave out the first `0`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop to 5 |number|:
        io.line([: [number]])
    ;
]

### Change the step

Use `by` to skip between values:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0 to 10 by 2 |number|:
        io.line([: [number]])
    ;
]

This produces `0`, `2`, `4`, `6` and `8`.

### Count downward

The direction comes from the bounds. You do not need a `reverse` keyword.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 5 to 0 |number|:
        io.line([: [number]])
    ;
]

Any range that uses `Float` needs an explicit step:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0.0 to 1.0 by 0.1 |amount|:
        io.line([: [amount]])
    ;
]

The value binding takes the range's numeric type, and the optional index binding
is always `Int`.

Fixed-width bounds and steps promote to a common type before the loop runs, and a
fixed binary float range also needs an explicit step. Fixed-width ranges are
accepted end-state and remain queued. See @../progress (Progress) for current
support.
